{DisplayName}: {ProjectName}
Build.ps1/.sh/.bat use the explicitly selected SDK, Contract and matched ToolsRoot.
C/Zig additionally select ZigRoot; Rust uses the existing pinned tool installer.
module.R4MF owns source, role, dependencies, resource names, entry and target.
Component versions are selected by the caller; no IDE/project selection state
is stored by the SDK. Technical exports require -Technical.

Use SDK Tools/New-Project.ps1 -TemplateId {TemplateId} -Directory <new path>
-ContractRoot <contract> -ToolsRoot <tools>. Rust's existing New-RustProject
command delegates to this same creator. The local R4L contract/baseline and
bindings are generated only during initial creation, with fresh interface IDs.
Change them later through the original library-contract generator/checks.

Resources and callback backing stay with their exact owner until actual close.
Stop/timeout/Drop alone do not prove asynchronous or hardware completion.
Driver templates are real R4D entry shells; supply device matching, admission,
DMA/IRQ/reset and retirement in the driver owner. Default misc selects no
physical device. Resident R4P templates close their own role explicitly through
op1; the current loader does not automatically invoke ProtocolShutdown/unload.
Subsystem templates provide the canonical host/launch/window shell; implement
its actual guest decoder with the shared video/audio/runtime/persistence API.
GUI starts through Desktop, services through SERVMAN, lowlevel keeps its raw
context entry. No direct Rust SIMD/FP or physical hardware readiness is implied.
